research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26477-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26478-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26479-**Refuted claim:** the monotonicity hypothesis in Dini's theorem can be dropped. For $k\in\mathbb{N}$ put $a_k:=\iota(k+1)$ and define the triangular spike $$h_k(x):=\max\{0,\ 1-|2a_kx-1|\}\qquad(0\le x\le1).$$ Each $h_k$ is continuous and $h_k\to0$ pointwise, but the convergence is not uniform.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26480-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:26481:cex-m-over-m-plus-n-has-noncommuting-iterated-limits
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26482-**Refuted claim:** whenever both iterated limits of a double real sequence exist, they are equal. For $m,n\in\mathbb{N}$ define $$s_{m,n}:=\frac{\iota(m+1)}{\iota(m+n+2)}=\frac{\iota(m+1)}{\iota(m+1)+\iota(n+1)}.$$ Then $$\lim_m\lim_ns_{m,n}=0,\qquad \lim_n\lim_ms_{m,n}=1.$$ The shifts make the expression defined at the first index $(m,n)=(0,0)$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26483-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26484-cex-uniform-convergence-does-not-control-derivatives
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-26485-**Refuted claim:** if differentiable functions converge uniformly, their derivatives must converge to the derivative of the limit. For $k\in\mathbb{N}$ put $a_k:=\iota(k+1)$ and define $$f_k(x):=\frac{x}{1+a_k^2x^2}\qquad(x\in\mathbb{R}).$$ Then $f_k\to0$ uniformly on $\mathbb{R}$, but $f_k'(0)=1$ for every $k$, whereas the derivative of the zero function is $0$.
